---
/**
 * Careers hero — Figma node 14052:30126.
 *
 * Centred headline with a rail of photo cards bleeding off each edge.
 *
 * SIZE BELONGS TO THE POSITION, NOT TO THE CARD. The slot nearest the copy is
 * always the smallest, the next one out is bigger, the one at the viewport edge
 * is biggest — whichever photo happens to be passing through. So the rail always
 * reads as the static Figma composition even though everything in it is moving:
 * a card enters at the edge full size and shrinks the whole way in, and the
 * shape of the group never changes.
 *
 * That is the one thing a plain marquee cannot do. Give each card a fixed size
 * and the sizes travel WITH the cards, so the composition churns and the rail
 * pulses on the repeat. Here every card is the same box and the shrinking is a
 * separate animation keyed to where the card is.
 *
 * HOW THE TWO ANIMATIONS STAY IN LOCK STEP, WITH NO SCRIPT. `.cp-hero-track`
 * slides the whole strip; `.cp-hero-card` scales each card. They are different
 * elements, so the transforms compose instead of fighting.
 *
 * Both run for exactly `--cp-rail-duration`, in which the strip advances one
 * six-card set — 6 × 220px = 1320px. So one turn of the scale animation is worth
 * 1320px of travel, and a card's scale can be read as a function of DISTANCE
 * from the lane's outer edge rather than of time. Each card is started part-way
 * through that turn, by exactly as far as it already sits down the strip:
 *
 *     animation-delay = −(slot ÷ 6) × duration        slot = --cp-i, 0…5
 *
 * Every card then traces the same scale-versus-position curve, one after
 * another, which is what makes the size stick to the place rather than to the
 * card. `--cp-i` repeats every 6 because the strip is the same 6 cards twice.
 *
 * THE SCALE STEPS ARE THE FIGMA SIZES, EXACTLY. 1 → 0.85 → 0.7225 applied to the
 * 221px card gives 221 / 187.85 / 159.673 — the three heights in the Figma frame,
 * which are themselves a 0.85 step. Better still, `scale()` takes the border and
 * padding with it (1px → 0.85px → 0.723px), and those are precisely the values
 * Figma draws at each step. The static frame needed three hand-written
 * geometries to express that; here one geometry plus a scale reproduces all
 * three and every position in between.
 *
 * The cost is that a scaled 1px border is a sub-pixel border, so the hairline
 * softens slightly at the small end. Figma's own smaller steps are sub-pixel too
 * (0.85px, 0.723px), so this is the design's own trade, not a new one.
 *
 * CARDS DISSOLVE INTO THE COPY, THEY ARE NOT CUT OFF. Each lane carries a mask
 * that fades to nothing just before the headline — see `.cp-hero-rail--left`.
 * Without it a card meets the copy box and is sliced by a hard vertical edge,
 * which is the one thing that gives the whole effect away.
 *
 * Figma steps opacity down with size as well (1 / 0.8 / 0.4). That is the same
 * fade, drawn per card because a still frame has no other way to say it. Here it
 * is a property of the POSITION, like the size: whichever card is nearest the
 * copy is the one dissolving, and it can be half dissolved, which a per-card
 * opacity could never show.
 *
 * Artwork is still the Figma "Visual Placeholder" tint — no photography exists
 * for this page yet. Put Webflow asset URLs in `LEFT_RAIL` / `RIGHT_RAIL` below
 * and each card renders an <img>; the boxes already carry the final dimensions.
 * Never a `data:` URI — one base64 photo can exceed the whole 50 KB Embed budget
 * on its own, and there are twelve slots here.
 *
 * Rails are hidden below 1280px: at 416px of centred copy they start colliding
 * with the headline, and there is nothing to gain from a half-visible card
 * sitting under the text.
 */
const HEADING_LINES = ["Make an Impact", "That Matters"];
const LEDE =
  "Join our team and contribute to shaping the future of millions of Indians and businesses worldwide.";
const CTA_LABEL = "View current openings";
const CTA_HREF = "https://clear.darwinbox.in/ms/candidatev2/main/careers/home";

/* Six images a side. Slots only — put a Webflow asset URL in one and that card
   renders an <img>; an empty string leaves the Figma placeholder tint.
 *
 * Six is not decorative. The strip is this set twice over, and the loop
 * translates by exactly one set, so changing the COUNT means recomputing the
 * keyframe stops in `@keyframes cp-card-depth` and the `÷ 6` in the card's
 * animation-delay. Changing the IMAGES costs nothing. */
const LEFT_RAIL: string[] = ["", "", "", "", "", ""];
const RIGHT_RAIL: string[] = ["", "", "", "", "", ""];

/* Doubled, so translating the strip by one set puts the clone exactly where the
   original was. Six is the editable set; twelve is what renders. */
const LEFT_TRACK: string[] = [...LEFT_RAIL, ...LEFT_RAIL];
const RIGHT_TRACK: string[] = [...RIGHT_RAIL, ...RIGHT_RAIL];

/* One geometry for every card — the largest Figma step. The other two steps are
   produced by `transform: scale()` as the card travels; see the note above. */
const CARD_CLASS =
  "cp-hero-card shrink-0 overflow-hidden rounded-[12px] border border-[#CCCCCC] bg-generic-00 p-2";
const MEDIA_CLASS =
  "cp-hero-media h-[221px] w-[170px] overflow-hidden rounded-lg bg-visual-placeholder";
---

<section
  class="cp-hero relative overflow-hidden border-b border-[#CCCCCC] bg-generic-00"
>
  <!-- Left rail. Travels RIGHT, towards the centre, shrinking as it goes.
       `--cp-i` is the card's slot in the six-card set, counted from the lane's
       OUTER edge — it is the card's head start through the scale curve, and it
       is the only thing that differs between the twelve cards here. -->
  <div
    class="cp-hero-rail cp-hero-rail--left absolute top-1/2 hidden -translate-y-1/2 xl:block"
    aria-hidden="true"
  >
    <div class="cp-hero-track">
      {
        LEFT_TRACK.map((image, i) => (
          <div class={`${CARD_CLASS} mr-8`} style={`--cp-i:${i % 6}`}>
            <div class={MEDIA_CLASS}>
              {image && (
                <img
                  src={image}
                  alt=""
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                  class="h-full w-full object-cover"
                />
              )}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Right rail — the mirror, so it travels LEFT. The strip is pinned to the
       lane's right edge and the slot index counts back from the last card, so
       `--cp-i` still measures outward-edge-first and both rails can share one
       curve. The margin flips to the left for the same reason: it has to trail
       each card on the side it came from. -->
  <div
    class="cp-hero-rail cp-hero-rail--right absolute top-1/2 hidden -translate-y-1/2 xl:block"
    aria-hidden="true"
  >
    <div class="cp-hero-track">
      {
        RIGHT_TRACK.map((image, i) => (
          <div class={`${CARD_CLASS} ml-8`} style={`--cp-i:${(11 - i) % 6}`}>
            <div class={MEDIA_CLASS}>
              {image && (
                <img
                  src={image}
                  alt=""
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                  class="h-full w-full object-cover"
                />
              )}
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </div>

  <div
    class="container relative mx-auto flex min-h-[520px] flex-col items-center justify-center py-20 text-center xl:min-h-[720px]"
  >
    <div class="cp-hero-copy flex flex-col items-center gap-6">
      <h1
        class="m-0 font-heading font-semibold text-vault-navy text-[length:var(--fs-display-lg)] leading-[length:var(--lh-display-lg)] tracking-[-1px]"
      >
        {HEADING_LINES[0]}<br />{HEADING_LINES[1]}
      </h1>

      <div class="flex w-full flex-col items-center gap-4">
        <p
          class="m-0 font-body text-neutral-500 text-[length:var(--fs-body-lg)] leading-[length:var(--lh-body-lg)]"
        >
          {LEDE}
        </p>
        <a
          href={CTA_HREF}
          class="cp-btn inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-lg border border-nova-blue bg-nova-blue px-5 py-3.5 font-body font-medium text-nova-50 text-[length:var(--fs-label-lg)] leading-[length:var(--lh-label-lg)] hover:bg-generic-00 hover:text-nova-blue transition-all ease-in-out duration-150"
        >
          {CTA_LABEL}
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style is:global>

  .cp-hero-copy {
    /* THE BACKSTOP BEHIND THE CARDS, so it must never be transparent or
       animated. The lane masks now do the visible work — cards have already
       dissolved by the time they reach this box — but it is what the section
       falls back to wherever `mask-image` is unsupported, and it still covers
       the lane clip and the card transform reset. Keep it opaque.

       It deliberately has NO entrance of its own: it used to fade in, and an
       opacity below 1 here makes the whole subtree — background included —
       see-through, so the rails showed straight through the "mask". Anything
       added here must keep it opaque.

       Invisible by design: white on a white section, exactly as the Figma frame
       draws it. Nothing is rendered; it only occludes. */
    background: var(--color-generic-00, #fff);
    /* Figma's 416px is the CONTENT width and is preserved exactly — box-sizing
       is border-box site-wide, so 480 − 2×32 = 416. The padding and the
       max-width have to move together, 416 being the invariant, which is why
       both live here rather than as a `max-w-[416px]` utility in the markup.

       HALF THIS WIDTH IS `--cp-rail-fade`. The rails dissolve exactly where this
       box begins, so the two are one measurement: 480 wide and centred puts its
       edge 240px back from the page centre, and 240px is what the lane masks
       fade out over. Change this and change that, or the rails will either
       vanish early into blank space or still be visible when this box starts
       covering them — and covering a half-visible card is the hard edge the
       fade exists to remove.

       This used to be 640px, from when it was the only thing hiding the cards
       and needed slack on every side. The masks do that job now, so it is back
       to the width the text actually wants — which is also what lets the rails
       run as close to the headline as the Figma frame draws them. */
    max-width: 480px;
    padding-block: 36px;
    padding-inline: 32px;
  }

  /* THE LANE. A window on the moving strip, anchored to one viewport edge.

     `--cp-rail-bleed` is how far it extends PAST that edge. Cards enter here at
     full size, so it has to clear a full-size card (188px) or they would pop in
     at the edge rather than slide in from outside it.

     `--cp-rail-span` is one set of six slots: 6 × (188px card + 32px margin).
     It is the distance the strip covers in one `--cp-rail-duration`, which makes
     it the conversion factor between the scale keyframes and real position — see
     `@keyframes cp-card-depth`. Change the card size, the margin or the count
     and this and those stops all move together.

     THE INNER EDGE IS THE PAGE CENTRE, and that is load-bearing. `overflow:
     hidden` is required — without it the 2640px strip would run straight out the
     far side of the headline — but a hard clip is only invisible while something
     opaque covers it. `.cp-hero-copy` is 640px wide and centred, so the page
     centre is 320px inside it AT EVERY VIEWPORT WIDTH. Pinning the lane's inner
     edge to 50% is what makes that true everywhere rather than at one width.

     The `min()` caps the lane at one span. Past roughly 2160px of layout the
     page centre is further out than a card travels in one turn, and without the
     cap the scale reset — which is meant to happen past the clip — would drift
     back into view. The cap keeps the clip at or inside the reset point.

     It is a PERCENTAGE, not `50vw`, and that is not cosmetic. shared.css zooms
     `body` to --page-zoom between 768px and 1439.98px; `vw` keeps resolving
     against the unzoomed viewport while layout happens in zoomed space, so the
     old travel maths here needed an explicit `/ var(--page-zoom)` correction and
     a media query duplicating shared.css's own bounds. A percentage of the
     section is already in the same coordinate space as the element it sizes, so
     the zoom cancels and both are gone. */
  .cp-hero-rail {
    --cp-rail-bleed: 240px;
    --cp-rail-span: 1320px;

    /* One full pass of a six-card set — 1320px, so about 47px/s. Slow enough to
       read as a standing composition that drifts, not as a ticker. These cards
       are the only artwork on the section; the rail exists to show them, not to
       move things along. */
    --cp-rail-duration: 28s;

    /* How long the dissolve into the copy runs, and where it ends. HALF THE
       WIDTH OF `.cp-hero-copy` — the lane's inner edge is the page centre and
       that box is 480px wide and centred, so it begins 240px back from that
       edge, which is `100% - 240px` in the lane's own terms at every viewport
       width. The fade has to be finished by then: past that point the copy box
       is opaque, and an opaque box landing on a half-faded card is the hard edge
       this whole mechanism exists to remove. */
    --cp-rail-fade: 240px;

    /* The card's border-box height: 221 media + 2×8 padding + 2×1 border. Set
       explicitly because the strip inside is absolutely positioned and so
       contributes nothing to the lane's height. */
    height: 239px;
    width: min(calc(50% + var(--cp-rail-bleed)), var(--cp-rail-span));
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* THE DISSOLVE. Without this a card meets the copy and is simply cut off — a
     straight vertical edge slicing it in half beside the headline, which is the
     one thing that gives the whole illusion away. Figma fades the innermost card
     out instead, and this is that fade expressed as a position rather than as a
     per-card opacity: whichever card is there dissolves, exactly as its size is
     whatever the position says.

     It also retires two other hazards. The lane's hard `overflow` clip and the
     point where each card's transform snaps back for its next pass both sit
     inboard of here, and the mask is already fully transparent by then — so
     neither can surface even if the copy box above were narrowed.

     Masking rather than animating opacity is deliberate. Opacity would have to
     live on the card, which would make it a property of the CARD again — the
     exact thing this section is built to avoid — and a card is only ever partly
     across the fade, so a whole-card opacity could not render the half-dissolved
     edge the design shows.

     If `mask-image` is unsupported the section degrades to the old behaviour:
     the opaque copy box still covers the cards, just with a hard edge instead of
     a soft one. Nothing breaks, it is only less pretty. */
  .cp-hero-rail--left {
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(
      to right,
      #000 calc(100% - var(--cp-rail-fade) * 2),
      transparent calc(100% - var(--cp-rail-fade))
    );
    mask-image: linear-gradient(
      to right,
      #000 calc(100% - var(--cp-rail-fade) * 2),
      transparent calc(100% - var(--cp-rail-fade))
    );
  }

  /* The mirror: `to left` makes 100% the lane's LEFT edge, which is the inner
     one on this side, so both rails reuse the same two stops. */
  .cp-hero-rail--right {
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(
      to left,
      #000 calc(100% - var(--cp-rail-fade) * 2),
      transparent calc(100% - var(--cp-rail-fade))
    );
    mask-image: linear-gradient(
      to left,
      #000 calc(100% - var(--cp-rail-fade) * 2),
      transparent calc(100% - var(--cp-rail-fade))
    );
  }

  /* `--cp-dir` is which way "outwards" points for this rail, and it is what lets
     both sides share one depth curve: the gap correction in `cp-card-depth`
     always pulls a card back towards its own viewport edge, which is leftwards
     on the left and rightwards on the right. */
  .cp-hero-rail--left {
    --cp-dir: 1;
    left: calc(var(--cp-rail-bleed) * -1);
  }

  .cp-hero-rail--right {
    --cp-dir: -1;
    right: calc(var(--cp-rail-bleed) * -1);
  }

  /* THE STRIP. Two identical sets of six — 12 × (188 + 32) = 2640px — so one set
     is 1320px and translating by exactly that puts the clone where the original
     was, making the wrap a no-op.

     PINNED TO THE LANE'S OUTER EDGE, which is why it is positioned rather than
     in flow. Every card's `--cp-i` is its distance down the strip FROM THAT EDGE
     in slots, and the scale curve is written in those terms, so the right rail
     has to measure from its right edge or the two sides would shrink out of step
     with each other by however wide the viewport happens to be.

     -50% resolves against the strip's own rendered width, so it is exactly one
     set however the cards measure. What it does NOT survive is a missing
     trailing margin: `gap` puts space BETWEEN items and not after the last one,
     so a 12-item gapped strip is two sets minus one gap and -50% lands half a
     gap out — a 16px jump, once every cycle, forever. Hence `mr-8` / `ml-8` on
     every card instead.

     `max-content` because the lane is narrower than the strip and a flex row
     would otherwise be squeezed to fit. `shrink-0` on the cards covers the same
     ground, but only one of the two has to fail for the loop length to change
     and the wrap to show. */
  .cp-hero-track {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    width: max-content;
    will-change: transform;
    animation: cp-rail-right var(--cp-rail-duration) linear infinite;
  }

  .cp-hero-rail--left .cp-hero-track {
    left: 0;
  }

  /* Both rails run INWARDS, so the two sides need opposite signs. The left lane
     pushes its strip right until the clone has taken the original's place; the
     right lane pulls its strip left. Linear, with no ease at either end — an
     eased loop has a visible pulse once per cycle, which is exactly the thing a
     seamless rail is trying not to have. */
  .cp-hero-rail--right .cp-hero-track {
    right: 0;
    animation-name: cp-rail-left;
  }

  @keyframes cp-rail-right {
    from {
      transform: translateX(-50%);
    }
    to {
      transform: translateX(0);
    }
  }

  /* BOTH RANGES END AT 0, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS THE LANE FULL. A strip only
     extends AWAY from the edge it is pinned to, so each one has to START one set
     outside its lane and slide in — never start flush and slide off. The left
     strip hangs off to the left (`-50%`), the right strip off to the right
     (`50%`); either way the lane is covered for the whole turn, and landing on 0
     is the same phase one set along, so the wrap is still a no-op.

     Writing this side as `0 → -50%` is the mirror of the SIGN but not of the
     GEOMETRY: pinned right, it walks the strip's outer end 1320px inwards and
     empties the lane from the viewport edge in, until the last card is sitting
     by the headline with nothing behind it. Then it snaps back full. */
  @keyframes cp-rail-left {
    from {
      transform: translateX(50%);
    }
    to {
      transform: translateX(0);
    }
  }

  /* THE DEPTH CURVE. One turn of this is one `--cp-rail-span` of travel, so the
     stops below are positions, not moments — each one is a distance from the
     lane's outer edge divided by the 1320px span:

       0 … 240px   scale 1        the bleed: outside the viewport, full size
       240px       scale 1        18.18%  the card reaches the viewport edge
       460px       scale 0.85     34.85%  one slot in
       680px       scale 0.7225   51.52%  two slots in, fully shrunk
       680 … end   scale 0.7225   holds, then snaps back to 1 for the next pass

     240 / 460 / 680 are the bleed plus zero, one and two 220px slots, so the
     three Figma sizes land exactly one slot apart — which is the Figma
     composition, rebuilt as a function of position.

     THE SNAP FROM 100% BACK TO 0% IS DELIBERATE and is the only discontinuity in
     this section. It happens at 1320px from the outer edge, which the `min()` on
     the lane guarantees is at or beyond the clip, and the clip is under
     `.cp-hero-copy`. So the card is already twice hidden when it resizes.

     A card that reaches full shrink before the copy simply holds there, which is
     why the curve flattens rather than continuing. On a narrow desktop the
     visible band is shorter than 680px and cards slip under the copy a little
     above 0.7225; that is a graceful loss of the last few percent, not a break.

     THE translateX IS NOT DECORATION — IT CLOSES THE GAPS. `scale()` shrinks
     what a card paints but not the 220px of layout it occupies, so a shrinking
     card leaves its slot half empty and the rail visibly spreads apart exactly
     where it should be tightening. Left alone, a 32px gap opens to 72px by the
     third slot and the small end reads as scattered rather than distant.

     The correction is the running shortfall: how far behind a card's painted
     width has fallen against its slot, accumulated from the viewport edge. In
     other words its visual position advances at the LOCAL SCALE rather than at
     a flat 220px a slot, which falls out as

         gap = 32px × (local scale)

     so cards and the spaces between them shrink by the same factor. That is what
     distance actually looks like, and it is a better answer than Figma's flat
     32px, which only has to hold for three cards that never move.

     The numbers are that running total at each stop: 0 at the edge, then 16.5,
     63.5 and 241.1px. They are integrals of the scale curve above — change a
     scale or a stop and every one of them moves. */
  .cp-hero-card {
    animation: cp-card-depth var(--cp-rail-duration) linear infinite;
    /* The head start: this card is already `--cp-i` slots down the strip, and
       there are 6 slots to a turn. Without it every card would shrink in unison
       and the whole strip would throb instead of the sizes staying put. */
    animation-delay: calc(var(--cp-rail-duration) * var(--cp-i, 0) / -6);
  }

  /* `translateX` before `scale` is deliberate: a CSS transform list applies
     left to right, so this scales the card about its own centre and THEN shifts
     it by an unscaled amount. Reversed, the shift would itself be scaled and the
     correction would come up short by exactly the factor it is correcting for. */
  @keyframes cp-card-depth {
    0%,
    18.18% {
      transform: translateX(0) scale(1);
    }
    34.85% {
      transform: translateX(calc(-16.5px * var(--cp-dir, 1))) scale(0.85);
    }
    51.52% {
      transform: translateX(calc(-63.5px * var(--cp-dir, 1))) scale(0.7225);
    }
    100% {
      transform: translateX(calc(-241.1px * var(--cp-dir, 1))) scale(0.7225);
    }
  }

  /* DO NOT move either animation onto `.cp-hero-rail`. `-translate-y-1/2` on the
     lane does NOT compile to a transform in Tailwind v4 — it compiles to the
     separate `translate` property, which composes with `transform` rather than
     being overridden by it. The three survive together only because they are on
     three different elements: the lane owns the vertical centring, the strip
     owns the horizontal loop, the card owns its scale. */

  /* Reduced motion PAUSES rather than cancelling. `animation: none` would drop
     every card back to scale 1 and leave a row of identical boxes; pausing
     freezes each card at the phase its delay already puts it in, so the rail
     stops on a stepped big-to-small composition — the Figma frame, held still. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .cp-hero-track,
    .cp-hero-card {
      animation-play-state: paused;
    }
  }
</style>
